First I would recommend reading some of Hale Landis' tutorials at
ATA-ATAPI.COM. That has some great information on the different ATA
addressing schemes and BIOS limitations. Then get a copy of ATA-1 and
see if you can't write a simple hard disk driver (implement IDENTIFY
DRIVE, READ SECTOR(S) and WRITE SECTOR(S)). Later, if you really want
to support advanced things like LBA and 48-bit addressing, you should
pick up the latest copy of the ATA specification (6 or 7). ATAPI is
SCSI commands over IDE wires (as Maxim put it to me some time ago).
You probably don't want to try that before you get your hard disk
driver working, so don't worry about it for now. One more thing to get
you started:

ATA Host Bus Adapter Defaults
----------------------------------------------
                  Primary            Secondary
IRQ:              14                 15
Command I/O Base: 1F0                170
Control I/O Base: 3F6                376

Please note that with control registers, only the byte at 3F6 or 376
(read or write -- 2 separate registers -- device control (W) and
alternate int. status (R)) is still used. The others are obsolete,
nevermind what the ATA-1 says.

Cheers,
Frank Millea

-------------------------------------------------------------------

 The basic method is:
   -wait until the device is ready (with polling or irq handling)
   -write out the address (in chs or lba)
   -write out the command
   -write until the data is ready (with polling or irq handling)
   -transfer the data (from/to the data port)

 There are more advanced methods too, that use bus mastering to avoid
 the transfer phase. (the controller transfers the data by itself)
